waterfall model : each phase is done one after another , we move to next phase only after completering the 
current stage. biggest drawback of this model is it incompartible with changing client requirement, 
adding new feature or any modification later on is not practical in this model . also it does not give temporary
software version which makes user requirement to be updated more confusing.

prototyping model : it allows the client to re-consider user requirement and add nnew suggestion
by looking a temporary software mini-version built in order to give user a gist of the final software to be made.
it gives room for improvement and user can reset the requirement.

iterative model : this model develops the software in a continues iterative way , the thing it does is making small-small improvement in each iteration , the iterations are not time bounded but rather feature bounded the 
development cycle goes like : build--deploy--improve+build---deploy---improve+build------deploy

sprial model : this model works in repeated improvement/iterations and continuous risk analysis.
main distinctive thing in spiral model is that it is much oriented with reliability . reduce possible system failure due to any future bug that is reason why with each improved iteration stage risk analysis also done to elimiate system fault.at the same time high-expertise are needed to predict future risks with any new proposed feature to be added . in nutshell appplying risk analysis is not cheap, small bussiness cannnot adopt this model as it is not feasible due to its high cost.


time-boxing model : model focus on time slots given to complete one unit of work , it is upto the team to decide what they want to prioritize first but changing time slot is non-negotiable , the scope of work within this given time can be changed but not hte time itself.
